<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Montenegro</h1><p class="st-meta">1516 CE – 1852 CE · capital Cetinje</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mountain theocracy ruled by Orthodox metropolitans of Cetinje, which kept Montenegro self-governing against the Ottomans for more than three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crnojevic-noble-family">Crnojević</a> rulers left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> in the 1490s and the land was absorbed into the Ottoman sanjak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scutari</a>, the metropolitans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a> became the recognised leaders of the highland clans; their secular role is traditionally dated from 1516. In 1697 the office passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danilo-i-metropolitan-of-cetinje">Danilo Petrović-Njegoš</a>, whose family kept it, usually from uncle to nephew.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The vladika was both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and prince, ruling a confederation of fiercely independent clans with little central administration, bound together by Orthodoxy and war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> onwards the prince-bishops sought Russian support. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petar-i-petrovic-njegos">Petar I</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-mahmud-pasha-bushati">Kara Mahmud Pasha Bushati</a> in 1796, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petar-ii-petrovic-njegos">Petar II</a>, author of the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-mountain-wreath">The Mountain Wreath</a>, created a senate and taxes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petar-ii-petrovic-njegos">Petar II</a> died in 1851, his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danilo-i-prince-of-montenegro">Danilo</a> refused ordination and in 1852, with Russian approval, proclaimed himself secular prince, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-montenegro">Principality of Montenegro</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-petrovic-njegos">Petrović-Njegoš</a> dynasty continued to rule until 1918, and the monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a> remains the seat of the Metropolitanate.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-montenegro.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Montenegro" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Desemeus · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Montenegro_(1516%E2%80%931852).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1516 CE – 1852 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cetinje</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Danilo I Petrović-Njegoš, Petar I Petrović-Njegoš, Petar II Petrović-Njegoš</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serbian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Old Montenegro, Metropolitanate of Cetinje (state)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1684&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-montenegro" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Montenegro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5139 670 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Montenegro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5459 926L5459 926M5459 926L5709 970M5459 926L5459 926"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5459 926h0M5709 970h0M5459 926h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5459 926h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5459 926h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cetinje. <a href="/atlas?year=1684&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-montenegro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
